be on Tuesday or Wednesday.\n   - If C is on Wednesday, then A and D must be two days apart, which means A can be on Thursday or Friday.\n   - If C is on Friday, then A and D must be two days apart, which means A can be on Saturday or Sunday, but this is not possible since A is only on duty from Monday to Friday.\n\n5. **Considering the possible days for A:**.\n   - If C is on Monday, then A can be on Tuesday or Wednesday.\n   - If C is on Wednesday, then A can be on Thursday or Friday.\n   - If C is on Friday, then A can be on Saturday or Sunday, but this is not possible since A is only on duty from Monday to Friday.\n\n6. **Combining the information:**.\n   - If B is on Monday, C cannot be on Monday, so C must be on Wednesday or Friday.\n   - If C is on Wednesday, then A can be on Thursday or Friday.\n   - If C is on Friday, then A can be on Saturday or Sunday, but this is not possible since A is only on duty from Monday to Friday.\n\n7. **Conclusion:**.\n   - The only possible scenario that satisfies all the constraints is when B is on Monday, C is on Wednesday, and A is on Thursday.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
