 safe limits.\n\nB) Humans have been ingesting the natural drugs contained in these plants for thousands of years and have time to adapt to them.\n- This option implies that humans have had enough time to develop resistance to the natural drugs contained in plants. However, this does not undermine the argument either. The argument states that humans have been ingesting these toxins without poisoning, which suggests that they have not developed resistance yet. If humans have been ingesting these toxins for thousands of years, it is possible that they have not developed resistance, and the argument still holds.\n\nC) The chemical structure of synthetic pesticides is usually simpler than that of natural drugs contained in plants.\n- This option suggests that synthetic pesticides are simpler in chemical structure than natural drugs. However, this does not necessarily undermine the argument. The argument states that plants produce chemicals that resist parasites, and humans ingest these toxins without poisoning. If synthetic pesticides are simpler in chemical structure, it would still be possible for humans to ingest them without poisoning, as long as the overall exposure is still within safe limits. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
